In the cryptocurrency ecosystem, there are three primary types of cryptocurrency exchanges. They differ based on who manages the funds, how trades are matched, and how you connect your fiat money (like XAF). [1] 
------------------------------
## 1. Centralized Exchanges (CEX)
A Centralized Exchange is operated by a single corporate entity that acts as a middleman. They hold your crypto in their own corporate wallets, use an "order book" to match buyers and sellers automatically, and require strict identity verification (KYC). [1, 2] 

* 
* How it works: You create an account with an email, upload your ID, and deposit funds. The exchange manages everything behind a user-friendly app dashboard.
* Examples: [Binance](https://p2p.binance.com/trade/UBAcameroun/USDT?fiat=XAF), [Bybit](https://www.bybit.com/en-GB/help-center/article/How-to-Sell-Crypto-with-P2P-Trading), [Coinbase](https://www.coinbase.com), and [Kraken](https://www.kraken.com). [1, 3, 4, 5] 
* 

## 2. Peer-to-Peer Marketplaces (P2P)
A P2P Exchange does not match trades automatically using an order book. Instead, it acts like an online classifieds marketplace (like a digital market board) where individual human buyers and human sellers interact directly. [1, 6] 

* 
* How it works: The platform acts strictly as an escrow agent. It locks the seller's crypto until the buyer sends payment via a local method (like MTN MoMo or Orange Money), then releases it. [6, 7] 
* Examples: Many large CEXs host built-in P2P sections (like Binance P2P or Bybit P2P), while others are standalone P2P platforms like [NoOnes](https://blog.noones.com/binance-p2p-alternative-cameroon). [5, 8] 
* 

## 3. Decentralized Exchanges (DEX)
A Decentralized Exchange has no companies, corporate servers, or middlemen. It runs entirely on open-source code called smart contracts directly on a blockchain network. [1] 

* 
* How it works: You do not create an account or provide an ID. Instead, you connect a private software wallet (like Trust Wallet) directly to the platform and swap one crypto asset for another directly out of your own possession.
* Important: You cannot use local cash or mobile money on a DEX. You can only trade crypto-to-crypto.
* Examples: [Uniswap](https://uniswap.org) (on Ethereum), PancakeSwap (on BNB Chain), and Raydium (on Solana). [1, 3, 8, 9] 
* 

------------------------------
## Direct Comparison Overview

| Feature | Centralized (CEX) | Peer-to-Peer (P2P) | Decentralized (DEX) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Who holds your crypto? | The Exchange | The Exchange Escrow | You (In your own wallet) |
| Accepts MoMo / XAF Cash? | Yes (via their P2P tabs) | Yes, natively | No (Crypto-to-crypto only) |
| Requires ID Upload (KYC)? | Yes | Yes | No (Total privacy) |
| Best used for... | Day trading & staking | Cashing out crypto to local mobile money | Advanced Web3 & Privacy |

